  - decision-curve-analysis
abstract: >-
  PulseShift forecasts whether weather and air quality will suppress a
  planned outdoor session in Washington DC and recommends the safest way
  to keep it, served as a calibrated model that runs in the browser. The
  outcome is outdoor mobility, proxied by bike-share ridership. Its
  evidence layer shows that the daily air-quality association with
  mobility is largely seasonal confounding: a within-day fixed-effects
  analysis on hourly data collapses the apparent effect toward zero,
  while calibration — not discrimination — governs whether the forecast
  can be trusted.
